              "summary": "**Evaluating survey confidence**\n\nI realize that the survey accuracy is .65 yielding a result of .718, but is confidence based on all 18 surveys? No, it seems .685715 might be a rounded figure from the probability after four surveys. With three successes out of four or five, using Bayes, the confidence could be noisy and shouldn't be guaranteed. I need to consider signals from the answers instead, and how different analysts could report their recommendations could affect my interpretation.**Analyzing confidence in forecasts**\n\nI think confidence might be treated as the stated probability, possibly reflecting a peer's probabilistic assessment. Combining forecasts seems like a good idea, but there’s this question about counting observations accurately. If confidence of .685715 relates to the forecast after observing three reports, then the local posterior would need to align correctly with those observations. I wonder if the confidence level of .685715 brings in uncertainty since the prompt states it’s subjective and not guaranteed.**Considering analyst observations**\n\nI'm thinking that if every analyst starts with three distinct surveys, maybe the reports they generate include additional info seen later on. There’s no specific order mentioned in how they analyze these reports. It seems the peer confidence values might be influenced by their local counts and could end up at .685715 based on a formula that takes into account social information. I wonder if I could compute from their answers using a majority threshold based on the likelihood of the answers relative to the state.",
